///1SYSVIEW
SYSVIEW.HLP - VT100/ANSI SYSTEM HARDWARE OVERVIEW

Select a topic below. Press Return to move up one level.
From CP/M: HELP SYSVIEW

SYSVIEW displays a compact hardware overview and waits for a key.
This patched CP/M-86 version uses a VT100/ANSI terminal through the
CP/M console instead of writing directly to an IBM PC display.

Start: SYSVIEW
Refresh: R
Exit to CP/M: Q or Esc

The original SYSVIEW.DOC is included in full under ORIGINAL.
Its IBM-display claims describe the original program, not this patch.

///2STARTING
START SYSVIEW

Copy the patched SYSVIEW.CMD and this SYSVIEW.HLP to your CP/M-86
disk. Keep a separate copy of the original program if you need it.

At the CP/M prompt:
  SYSVIEW

Use a VT100/ANSI terminal with at least 80 columns and 24 rows.
The program clears the terminal and displays the hardware overview.
There are no command-line options or configuration files.

To read this help:
  HELP SYSVIEW

SYSVIEW.CMD is a CP/M-86 program, not a DOS or CP/M-80 executable.
A serial terminal or terminal emulator can display its console output.

///2KEYS
REFRESH AND EXIT

  R or r       Read the information again and redraw the screen.
  Q or q       Exit SYSVIEW and return to CP/M.
  Esc          Exit SYSVIEW and return to CP/M.

No Return is needed after a command key.
Other ordinary character keys do not change the displayed values.
Cursor keys are not required; some send sequences beginning with Esc
and may therefore cause the program to exit.

Exit restores normal text attributes and makes the cursor visible.
The screen is not continuously refreshed. Use R when you want another
snapshot. No speaker warning or animated exit is used in this edition.

///2DISPLAY
READ THE OVERVIEW

Fields shown by the terminal edition:
  Processor class         Broad CPU compatibility class.
  IBM ROM date field      Eight characters from the IBM ROM date area.
  Math coprocessor        Flag recorded in the BIOS equipment word.
  Conventional memory     Memory size recorded in the BIOS data area.
  Guest BIOS video mode   Numeric BIOS mode, not the terminal type.
  Floppy drives           Count encoded in the BIOS equipment word.
  Parallel/LPT ports      Count encoded in the BIOS equipment word.
  Game port               Flag recorded in the BIOS equipment word.
  Secondary video         Not probed by this terminal edition.

These are reported values, not a comprehensive hardware test.
A Yes flag does not prove that a device has been functionally tested.
A guest emulator can supply partial, synthetic or missing BIOS data.

///2PROCESSOR
PROCESSOR CLASS

The display distinguishes broad historical FLAGS-register classes:
  8086/8088 compatible
  80286
  80386 or later

The first category includes NEC V20/V30-compatible behaviour.
The terminal edition does not identify a V20 separately.
The last category does not identify an exact later processor model,
clock speed, vendor or modern CPU features.

In an emulator, the result describes its emulated CPU behaviour.
It does not identify the physical processor in your Mac or host PC.
CPU classification depends on the emulator implementing FLAGS
behaviour consistently with the historical processor class.

///2BIOSDATA
BIOS DATA AND MISSING INFORMATION

The terminal edition does not call IBM BIOS interrupts to obtain
its display or keyboard input. Console I/O uses CP/M BDOS.

It reads conventional PC BIOS data at segment 0040h:
  offset 0010h   equipment word
  offset 0013h   conventional-memory size in KiB
  offset 0049h   video-mode byte

The memory-size word is used as a simple plausibility check.
If it is zero or greater than 1024, the related fields display:
  Not reported (no valid IBM BIOS data area)

This check cannot prove that every field is accurate. A non-PC CP/M
system may not maintain these addresses at all, and an emulator may
leave valid-looking but incomplete information there.
Missing information is not the same as a verified missing device.

///2ROMDATE
IBM ROM DATE FIELD

SYSVIEW reads eight bytes at F000:FFF5, the conventional IBM-compatible
ROM date location. Non-printable bytes are displayed as question marks.

A date-looking string belongs to the guest firmware image.
It is not the current date, CP/M installation date or host date.
On a non-IBM system or emulator without that ROM layout, the field
may contain question marks or unrelated printable characters.
The program does not validate that the string is a genuine date.

///2MEMORY
CONVENTIONAL MEMORY

The memory figure is the conventional-memory count stored by the
BIOS in its data area. One KiB is 1024 bytes.

It is not a measurement of:
  free CP/M transient program space
  available heap space in a running application
  extended or expanded memory
  installed RAM in the host computer

A value such as 640 KiB describes the conventional guest memory
reported by its firmware data. SYSVIEW does not test RAM contents
or probe physical memory to determine how much is installed.

///2DEVICES
DEVICE FLAGS AND COUNTS

Math coprocessor:
  Uses equipment-word bit 1. No floating-point instructions are run
  to test whether the reported coprocessor works.

Floppy drives:
  If the floppy-present bit is set, bits 6-7 encode a count of 1-4.
  Otherwise the displayed count is zero. No disk is accessed.

Parallel/LPT ports:
  Uses the count encoded in equipment-word bits 14-15.
  No printer is contacted and no test page is sent.

Game port:
  Uses equipment-word bit 12. No joystick hardware is probed.

The fields are withheld if the BIOS-data plausibility check fails.
Emulated CP/M disk drives need not correspond to PC floppy hardware.
For example, several mounted disk images can coexist with a BIOS
floppy count of zero if that is how the emulator supplies its data.

///2VIDEO
VIDEO MODE AND REMOTE TERMINAL

Guest BIOS video mode is the numeric byte stored in the BIOS data
area. It describes the guest's recorded display mode, not the ANSI
terminal connected to the CP/M console.

For example, decimal mode 3 conventionally denotes a PC text mode.
The terminal can still be a VT100/ANSI emulator connected remotely.
SYSVIEW does not change that BIOS mode.

Secondary video is deliberately shown as Not probed.
The original low-level video-memory and display-controller probes
are not executed in this edition. They are unsuitable as a general
way to interrogate a remote terminal or a minimal emulator.

///2TERMINAL
TERMINAL REQUIREMENTS

Use ANSI/VT100 emulation, at least 80 columns and 24 rows.
The program uses these terminal operations:
  ESC [ 0 m        Normal text attributes.
  ESC [ 2 J        Clear the display.
  ESC [ H          Move cursor to the upper-left corner.
  ESC [ ? 25 l     Hide cursor while showing the overview.
  ESC [ ? 25 h     Show cursor on exit.
  ESC [ 24 ; 1 H   Position for returning to the command prompt.

Spaces above separate parts for readability; they are not transmitted.
A terminal without cursor-hide support can ignore those two sequences.
Ordinary text uses ASCII with carriage-return/line-feed line endings.

If literal bracket codes appear, check the terminal's emulation mode.
If the display wraps, check its column count. If keys do not arrive,
check the CP/M console connection and the terminal input settings.

///2CHANGES
WHAT THE VT100/ANSI PATCH CHANGES

The startup branch now enters a replacement terminal front end.
The original hardware-display path is retained in the binary but
is not executed by the patched startup path.

Removed from the executed path:
  direct writes to IBM video memory
  private IBM console control codes and BIOS keyboard input
  screen animations and speaker/timer effects
  IBM BIOS interrupt calls
  active CRT-controller and video-memory probes

Added:
  ANSI display through CP/M BDOS direct console output
  keyboard input through CP/M BDOS direct console input
  R to refresh and Q as an additional exit key
  checks for missing BIOS data and printable ROM-field characters

The executable's load-group length and minimum allocation were
expanded to include the added code. The original file was not edited
in place during preparation; the patched file is supplied separately.

///2TROUBLE
COMMON QUESTIONS

It shows Not reported for most hardware.
  The guest may not maintain a plausible IBM BIOS data area.
  The terminal interface still works; it cannot invent device data.

It does not report my Mac's hardware.
  Correct: SYSVIEW runs inside CP/M-86 and examines the guest.

The floppy count differs from the number of CP/M drives.
  Those are different things. The count comes from PC BIOS flags,
  while CP/M can expose virtual disks in many other ways.

Why is secondary video no longer identified?
  Its original active hardware probes are bypassed for compatibility.

Why is a V20 shown as 8086/8088 compatible?
  The replacement uses broad FLAGS-based classification rather than
  the original processor-specific instruction/timing test.

The cursor remains hidden after an abnormal interruption.
  Use your terminal's reset function. A normal Q or Esc exit restores
  cursor visibility and normal text attributes.

Can I redirect this to a plain-text log?
  The output contains ANSI screen-control sequences. Use a terminal
  capture that understands ANSI or remove those sequences afterwards.

///2ORIGINAL
ORIGINAL SYSVIEW.DOC - COMPLETE TEXT

The following text is preserved from the document supplied by you.
It describes the original IBM-display version. Hardware probing and
terminal behaviour differ in the patch; see CHANGES and BIOSDATA.
The original web address is retained as a historical reference.

SYSVIEW  Version 0.1  [For IBM-Compatibles]
System Hardware Overview
Freeware from Kirk Lawrence

SYSVIEW gives you a quick "inside" look at an IBM-compatible computer.  It's
handy for scoping out unknown systems, or for confirming the hardware in a
known computer.

SYSVIEW identifies, and reports on the screen, various aspects of a computer's
hardware content.

SYSVIEW is a "work-in-progress," and future updates can be expected.
Please report any bugs through The CP/M-86 Software Repository web site
(http://www.seanet.com/~klaw)

SYSVIEW runs on =any= IBM-compatible computer.  It is fully compatible with
every type of video system...from MDA monochrome upwards.

SYSVIEW.CMD is free.  As is always the case with "freeware," there are no
guarantees whatsoever.  If you use the program, you do so entirely at your
own risk and peril.  Your use of SYSVIEW.CMD shall be prima facie evidence
under law that you understand and accept these terms and conditions.

///2TESTING
PATCH VALIDATION

The patched 8086 code was exercised under QEMU with a simulated
CP/M BDOS console and controlled BIOS-data memory fixtures.
Tests checked initial display, refresh, Q/q/Esc exit, cursor restore,
normal device counts, zero device flags and missing BIOS data.
Any attempted PC BIOS video/keyboard/equipment/time interrupt in
these tests would cause a failure.

The tests did not boot your actual CP/M-86 disk or verify every CPU
model. Hardware results depend on the data your guest supplies.

The accompanying patch source and rebuild script document the changes.
The supplied SYSVIEW.CMD is ready to copy; no assembler is needed
merely to use it.

HELP format: ///1 root and ///2 topics, ASCII text, CRLF line endings
and trailing Ctrl-Z padding to a multiple of 128 bytes.
